    <h1>Plan overview</h1>
    <p class="sub">The story behind the numbers on the dashboard — what this plan is trying to achieve, where retirement income comes from, the assumptions behind the projections, and how confident we can be in the result.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="card">
  <div class="cardhead">
    <div>
      <h2>About this plan</h2>
      <p class="desc">A financial plan exists to answer a simple question: is there enough, and will it last? This one is built around a comfortable, flexible retirement rather than a bare-minimum one.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>The core goal is a secure retirement that still leaves room for the things that make it worth it — travel, camping, time with family, and the flexibility to replace a vehicle or handle an unexpected cost without it becoming a crisis. Day-to-day living expenses are planned to stay steady in real terms, with a separate, larger allowance for travel and discretionary spending in the earlier, more active retirement years, tapering off later on.</p>
  <p>The plan is reviewed and updated at least once a year, and whenever a major life event happens — that's normal and expected, not a sign anything is off track. The numbers on the dashboard reflect the most current version.</p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <div class="cardhead">
    <div>
      <h2>Where retirement income comes from</h2>
      <p class="desc">Retirement income is layered from several sources, each started at a deliberately chosen time rather than all at once.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Government benefits (CPP and OAS).</strong> Aubrey began CPP early, at 60. Darla is holding off on hers until 70 — delaying increases the payment amount, so the tradeoff is a smaller income now for a larger, permanently higher one later. Both are also postponing Old Age Security (OAS) past the standard age for the same reason: every year of delay meaningfully increases the payment, and postponing avoids clawbacks while there's still other income. Between CPP and OAS, government benefits are expected to provide roughly $35,000–$36,000 a year in today's dollars, adjusted for inflation for as long as either of them is alive.</p>
  <p><strong>Annuities.</strong> Aubrey and Darla have each purchased an annuity, together paying out close to $14,000 a year starting in mid-2028. Unlike CPP and OAS, these payments are fixed rather than inflation-adjusted, and they continue in full as long as either of them is living — the purpose is to guarantee a baseline of income no matter how long retirement lasts, which is exactly the kind of risk (living longer than expected) that's hardest to plan around otherwise.</p>
  <p><strong>Personal savings.</strong> Whatever government benefits and annuities don't cover comes from personal accounts, in a deliberate order: non-registered (taxable) savings first, then the required minimum RRSP withdrawals, and tax-free TFSA savings last — the same order the withdrawal chart on the dashboard shows. The reasoning is tax efficiency: RRSP withdrawals are taxable income, so spreading them evenly avoids pushing into a higher tax bracket in any one year, while letting the TFSA keep growing tax-free for as long as possible. Along the way, some of the taxable savings are redirected into the TFSAs each year rather than spent, which is why the TFSA balances keep climbing even while withdrawals are happening elsewhere.</p>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <div class="cardhead">
    <div>
      <h2>Planning assumptions</h2>
      <p class="desc">Every projection rests on assumptions about the future — inflation, investment returns, and how long the plan needs to last. These are chosen to be reasonable and a little conservative, not optimistic.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inflation:</strong> 3% a year, based on Statistics Canada's long-term historical average. Recent inflation has often run lower than that, but the plan doesn't count on that continuing.</li>
    <li><strong>Investment growth:</strong> the portfolio is globally diversified across thousands of companies through low-cost index funds, with a modest tilt toward Canadian holdings. The expected long-run return is roughly 7% a year for the growth (equity) portion and roughly 3.4% for the stable (fixed-income/GIC) portion — with the understanding that any single year can vary well above or below that average.</li>
    <li><strong>How long the plan needs to last:</strong> the projections plan for Aubrey to age 87 and Darla to age 94. Planning past a "typical" life expectancy is intentional — it's better for the plan to comfortably outlast a long life than to run short.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="card">
  <div class="cardhead">
    <div>
      <h2>Confidence and key takeaways</h2>
      <p class="desc">Because markets don't move in a straight line, the plan isn't tested against a single projection — it's stress-tested against thousands of simulated versions of the future.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>That process (a Monte Carlo simulation) puts the probability of the plan fully funding every planned expense at roughly 85–92%, depending on the exact retirement timing — and importantly, trimming planned expenses by just 10% would push that well above 90% in every scenario tested. That gap is the plan's built-in safety margin: there's real flexibility to retire a little earlier, spend a little more freely, or absorb a rough few years in the market without the plan being in danger.</p>
  <p>A few things worth keeping in mind going forward:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The plan is well-funded as it stands, with room to either retire earlier or spend more comfortably — not just meet the bare minimum.</li>
    <li>Keeping an eye on actual spending versus the plan is the single biggest lever for staying confident in it; the plan is a guide, not a fixed script.</li>
    <li>The annuities exist specifically to protect against the risk of a long life outlasting savings — that's already built in, not something to worry about separately.</li>
    <li>The single biggest risk to the plan is the early loss of Aubrey's CPP and OAS income, since it makes up a large share of guaranteed income. That's the reason his life insurance is intended to stay in place rather than be cashed out — it exists to cover exactly that gap.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="foot">This page summarizes the household's financial plan as prepared by MacLeod Financial Associates. Figures are rounded and, in places, aggregated across accounts for this overview. It's a summary for context, not a substitute for the full plan — and like the dashboard, it's an illustration based on stated assumptions, not a guarantee.</p>
</section>
